
Get a list of deployments

### Expanding the deployment collection response

LaunchDarkly supports expanding the deployment collection response to include additional fields.

To expand the response, append the expand query parameter and include the following:

• pullRequests includes details on all of the pull requests associated with each deployment
• flagReferences includes details on all of the references to flags in each deployment

For example, use ?expand=pullRequests to include the pullRequests field in the response. By default, this field is **not** included in the response.

Usage:
  ldcli insights-deployments-beta list-deployments [flags]

Optional flags:
      --after string         Identifier used for pagination
      --application string   Comma separated list of application keys
      --before string        Identifier used for pagination
      --environment string   The environment key
      --expand string        Expand properties in response. Options:
                             'pullRequests', 'flagReferences'
      --from string          Unix timestamp in milliseconds. Default value
                             is 7 days ago.
      --kind string          The deployment kind
      --limit string         The number of deployments to return. Default
                             is 20. Maximum allowed is 100.
      --project string       The project key
      --status string        The deployment status
      --to string            Unix timestamp in milliseconds. Default value
                             is now.

Global flags:
  -h, --help                  Get help about any command
      --access-token string   LaunchDarkly access token with write-level access
      --analytics-opt-out     Opt out of analytics tracking
      --base-uri string       LaunchDarkly base URI (default "https://app.launchdarkly.com")
      --fields strings        Comma-separated list of top-level fields to include in JSON output (e.g., --fields key,name,kind)
      --json                  Output JSON format (shorthand for --output json)
  -o, --output string         Output format: json, plaintext, or markdown (default: plaintext in a terminal, json otherwise) (default "json")
